  <main id="main-content" class="poster">
    <div class="top-rule" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <section class="header animate" aria-labelledby="poster-title">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">Medical AI & Digital Health Conference 2024</div>
        <h1 id="poster-title">Large Language Models in Medicine</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">Opportunities, risks, and the path forward for clinical practice, biomedical research, and healthcare delivery.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="venue-box" aria-label="Poster context">
        <div class="kicker">Comprehensive Review</div>
        <p>From benchmark excellence to accountable clinical deployment</p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="key-message animate delay-1" aria-label="Key message">
      <strong>Key message:</strong> LLMs show remarkable capability in medical contexts, but the gap between benchmark performance and safe clinical deployment remains significant and demands a structured framework.
    </section>

    <section class="metrics animate delay-2" aria-label="Headline metrics">
      <article class="metric card">
        <div><div class="metric-value">&gt;90%</div><div class="metric-label">GPT-4 USMLE Score</div></div>
        <p class="metric-context">Exceeded the passing threshold on all three steps of the United States Medical Licensing Examination.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="metric card">
        <div><div class="metric-value">500+</div><div class="metric-label">PubMed Articles (2023)</div></div>
        <p class="metric-context">Peer-reviewed publications on LLMs in medicine appeared in 2023 alone.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="metric risk card">
        <div><div class="metric-value">Up to 30%</div><div class="metric-label">Hallucination Rate</div></div>
        <p class="metric-context">Medical outputs may include fabricated references or inaccurate clinical information.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="metric card">
        <div><div class="metric-value">$10.5B</div><div class="metric-label">Projected 2030 Market</div></div>
        <p class="metric-context">Estimated global healthcare LLM market by 2030.</p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <div class="grid-main">
      <section class="overview animate delay-3" aria-labelledby="overview-title">
        <article class="card">
          <h2 id="overview-title" class="section-title">What Are LLMs and Why Do They Matter?</h2>
          <p class="body-copy">Large language models such as GPT-4, Med-PaLM 2, and LLaMA are foundation models trained on vast corpora of text. In medicine, they can interpret clinical language, generate documentation, support education, and synthesize biomedical literature.</p>
          <div class="model-row" aria-label="Representative models">
            <div class="model-chip"><h3>GPT-4</h3><p>General-purpose model with strong medical benchmark results.</p></div>
            <div class="model-chip"><h3>Med-PaLM 2</h3><p>Medically tuned model approaching expert-level MedQA performance.</p></div>
            <div class="model-chip"><h3>LLaMA</h3><p>Open model family adapted for domain-specific biomedical workflows.</p></div>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h2 class="section-title">Adoption Context</h2>
          <p class="section-lede">The field has moved from experimentation to early clinical integration at unusual speed.</p>
          <p class="body-copy">Documentation and patient-facing communication tools have seen the fastest real-world adoption, including LLM-powered assistants integrated into electronic health records. Yet most evaluations still rely on controlled tasks, not prospective clinical trials.</p>
        </article>
      </section>

      <section class="applications card animate delay-4" aria-labelledby="applications-title">
        <h2 id="applications-title" class="section-title">Key Clinical and Research Applications</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">LLMs are being explored across a broad spectrum of medical tasks, with documentation and communication closest to routine deployment.</p>
        <div class="app-table" role="table" aria-label="LLM applications in medicine">
          <div class="app-row" role="row"><h3>Clinical Documentation</h3><p>Automated note generation and discharge summaries</p><span class="status">Pilot deployments</span></div>
          <div class="app-row" role="row"><h3>Diagnostic Support</h3><p>Differential diagnosis generation and radiology report interpretation</p><span class="status">Research stage</span></div>
          <div class="app-row" role="row"><h3>Patient Communication</h3><p>Answering patient questions and simplifying medical jargon</p><span class="status">Early adoption</span></div>
          <div class="app-row" role="row"><h3>Medical Education</h3><p>Exam preparation, tutoring, and case-based learning</p><span class="status">Active use</span></div>
          <div class="app-row" role="row"><h3>Biomedical Research</h3><p>Literature synthesis, hypothesis generation, protocol drafting</p><span class="status">Emerging</span></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="risk-card card animate delay-4" aria-labelledby="risks-title">
        <h2 id="risks-title" class="section-title">Risks, Limitations, and Ethical Concerns</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">Benchmark success does not eliminate safety, equity, privacy, or validation challenges.</p>
        <div class="risk-list">
          <article class="risk-item"><div class="risk-num">1</div><div><h3>Hallucinations</h3><p>Fabricated but plausible medical claims and fake citations can appear in up to 30% of outputs.</p></div></article>
          <article class="risk-item"><div class="risk-num">2</div><div><h3>Bias and Equity</h3><p>Training data can amplify disparities in care recommendations across demographic groups.</p></div></article>
          <article class="risk-item"><div class="risk-num">3</div><div><h3>Privacy</h3><p>Prompts may transmit patient data to external systems, raising HIPAA and GDPR concerns.</p></div></article>
          <article class="risk-item"><div class="risk-num">4</div><div><h3>Lack of Clinical Validation</h3><p>Most evaluations use multiple-choice benchmarks rather than prospective clinical trials.</p></div></article>
          <article class="risk-item"><div class="risk-num">5</div><div><h3>Over-reliance Risk</h3><p>Automation bias may lead clinicians to accept outputs without sufficient appraisal.</p></div></article>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="evidence animate delay-5" aria-label="Performance benchmarks and evidence">
      <article class="chart-card card">
        <h2 class="section-title">Performance Benchmarks</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">LLMs encode substantial medical knowledge, but real-world reasoning and safety are less characterized.</p>
        <div class="chart-wrap" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart comparing medical exam scores for GPT-3.5, Med-PaLM 2, GPT-4, and USMLE passing threshold">
          <canvas id="benchmarkChart" role="img" aria-label="GPT-4 scored above 90 percent on USMLE, Med-PaLM 2 scored 86.5 percent on MedQA, GPT-3.5 scored near 60 percent, and passing threshold is approximately 60 percent."></canvas>
        </div>
        <p class="chart-note">Standardized examinations are useful signals, but they do not substitute for clinical workflow evaluation.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="chart-card card">
        <h2 class="section-title">The Central Tension</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">High exam performance coexists with clinically meaningful failure modes.</p>
        <div class="benchmark-callouts">
          <div class="benchmark-callout"><div class="score">&gt;90%</div><div><h3>Knowledge benchmark strength</h3><p>GPT-4 exceeded the USMLE passing threshold across all three steps.</p></div></div>
          <div class="benchmark-callout"><div class="score" style="color: var(--negative);">30%</div><div><h3>Output reliability risk</h3><p>Hallucinations can include fabricated references or inaccurate clinical content.</p></div></div>
