        </div>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section class="framework animate delay-6" aria-labelledby="framework-title">
      <h2 id="framework-title" class="section-title">A Framework for Responsible Deployment</h2>
      <div class="framework-grid">
        <article class="frame-step"><div class="step">Step 01</div><h3>Prospective Evaluation</h3><p>Test in diverse clinical settings before deployment, not only on retrospective benchmarks.</p></article>
        <article class="frame-step"><div class="step">Step 02</div><h3>Human-in-the-Loop</h3><p>Ensure clinician oversight of all outputs and define clear accountability for decisions.</p></article>
        <article class="frame-step"><div class="step">Step 03</div><h3>Transparent Reporting</h3><p>Disclose limitations, training data sources, intended use, and known failure modes.</p></article>
        <article class="frame-step"><div class="step">Step 04</div><h3>Equity Audits</h3><p>Measure differential performance across populations and mitigate biased recommendations.</p></article>
        <article class="frame-step"><div class="step">Step 05</div><h3>Regulatory Alignment</h3><p>Align with evolving FDA, EMA, and WHO guidance for AI-based medical devices.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="footer" aria-label="Poster footer">
      <p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> LLMs hold transformative potential for medicine, but deployment must be guided by evidence, equity, and robust governance to ensure patient safety.</p>
      <p class="micro">Sources synthesized from the provided review brief and poster specification. Medical AI & Digital Health Conference 2024.</p>
    </section>
